   ... checked 34 good primes p<160;  violations of a_p=1+p mod 6: 0

THE MECHANISM (rigorous, classical):
  14a has torsion subgroup E(Q)_tors = Z/6Z (a RATIONAL point of order 6). A rational N-torsion
  point makes the mod-N Galois rep rho_N REDUCIBLE: rho_N ~ [[1,*],[0,chi]] (trivial subrep from
  the torsion point, cyclotomic quotient chi from the Weil pairing). Hence for good p:
     a_p = trace rho_N(Frob_p) = 1 + chi(Frob_p) = 1 + p  (mod N=6).
  This is EXACTLY the Eisenstein congruence: f_14 = E_2-Eisenstein mod 6 (the reducible = Eisenstein rep).

THE LINK to Phi_6 / Eisenstein (why the modulus is 6):
  6 = ord(zeta_6) = deg-structure of Phi_6, the 6TH CYCLOTOMIC polynomial Phi_6(x)=x^2-x+1,
  whose values Phi_6(n)=n^2-n+1 are the COVERING-MIN DENOMINATORS (n/Phi_6(n)). The mod-6 reduction
  lands in mu_6 = the 6th roots of unity = Z[zeta_6] (Eisenstein). So f_14 is congruent mod Phi_6's
  modulus to the Eisenstein series. And the COVERING-MIN side agrees (HYP-3768): the Dedekind margin
  s(n,Phi_6) -> -1/12 = the E_2 EISENSTEIN anomaly constant. BOTH f_14 and the covering-min reduce to
  the EISENSTEIN series -- f_14 via the mod-6 congruence, the covering-min via the E_2 anomaly.

CONCLUSION: Phi_6 (Eisenstein, j=0) and 14a (conductor 14) are DIFFERENT curves (S94, no functor)
but LINKED by the mod-6 = mod-Phi_6 EISENSTEIN CONGRUENCE a_p=1+p mod 6. Congruence, not functor --
the standard way different curves/forms link (Mazur's Eisenstein ideal). The Eisenstein series is the
common body; 14a is f_14's home, Phi_6 is the covering-min's home, and they are congruent mod 6.
